    "heading": "2. System Software [Spec 1.3.2 & 1.3.3]",
    "id": "h-2",
    "text": "2.1 The Operating System (OS) 📋 SPEC 1.3.2 / 1.3.3 Know about operating systems and utilities. Know OS functions: single user and network, memory management, resource management, security, print spooling. The Operating System (OS) is the most critical piece of system software. It acts as a bridge between the user, applications, and the physical hardware. Without an OS, no other software can run. 2.2 Single-User OS vs Network OS [Spec 1.3.3 — often missed!] This distinction is directly in the spec and frequently appears in exam scenario questions. Single-User OS Network OS Manages ONE user on ONE device Manages MANY users connected to a central server Used on home laptops and personal computers Used in schools, businesses, hospitals with many computers Simpler; fewer security controls required Complex; manages user accounts, access rights, policies for all User controls their own files and settings IT administrator controls access for the entire organisation Example: Windows 11 on a home laptop Example: Windows Server, Linux Server in a school network ✓ Easier to set up; low maintenance cost ✓ Centralised backup, security, and software management ✗ No shared resources; not suitable for organisations ✗ Needs skilled IT staff; expensive server hardware required ⚡ EXAM TIP Exam question: 'A secondary school has 300 computers all connected to a central server. Describe a suitable type of operating system and explain TWO benefits.' Answer: A NETWORK operating system (e.g. Windows Server). Benefits: (1) IT administrator can control user access rights centrally, preventing students from accessing restricted content. (2) Centralised backup means all student work is automatically saved to the server, so data is not lost if an individual computer fails. 2.3 The 9 OS Functions [Spec 1.3.3] ALL of the following functions must be known for the exam. Questions often ask for two or three functions with explanations — never just name them. 1 Memory Management Allocates RAM to programs; ensures they don't clash or overwrite each other e.g. Running Chrome + Word + Spotify simultaneously without crashing 2 Resource Management Controls access to shared hardware (CPU time, printers, disk read/write) among competing programs e.g. Print queue — only one program uses the printer at a time; CPU shared fairly 3 Security Management Manages user accounts, passwords, access permissions and authentication e.g. Password login screen; file permissions preventing access to other users' files 4 User Interface (UI) Provides the means by which users interact with the computer (GUI or CLI) e.g. Windows desktop with icons and menus (GUI); Linux Terminal with typed commands (CLI) 5 Device Management (Drivers) Uses device drivers to communicate with and control hardware peripherals e.g. OS detects USB printer and loads correct driver; screen resolution managed by GPU driver 6 Multitasking Runs multiple programs simultaneously by rapidly switching CPU between them e.g. Listening to Spotify while browsing and writing a document 7 Print Spooling Queues print jobs from multiple users/programs so the printer is not overwhelmed; users can keep working while jobs wait e.g. 3 students send print jobs; OS queues them — all continue working while printer processes in order 8 Error Handling Detects hardware or software faults and alerts the user or attempts automatic recovery e.g. 'Application not responding' dialog; Blue Screen of Death error report 9 File Management Organises files into folders; handles naming, saving, copying, moving and deletion of data e.g. Saving an essay to the 'Documents' folder; moving photos to the 'Pictures' library 2.4 Print Spooling — Visual Explanation Print spooling is specifically named in Spec 1.3.3 and is one of the most commonly missed points. Here is how it works: USERS SEND PRINT JOBS → OS PRINT SPOOL QUEUE ① Job from User 1 ② Job from User 2 ③ Job from User 3 → PRINTER Prints jobs one at a time in order All 3 users continue working while their jobs wait 💡 Why Print Spooling Is Resource Management Without spooling: User A must stand at their computer watching the printer finish User B's 30-page document before their own job prints. With spooling: OS stores all jobs in a queue on disk. All users can continue working. Printer processes jobs in order automatically. Link to spec: Print spooling is a specific form of RESOURCE MANAGEMENT — the OS manages the shared printer hardware efficiently across all users. 2.5 Utility Software [Spec 1.3.2] Utility software is a type of system software that maintains, optimises and protects the computer. Unlike the OS which always runs, utilities are called on when needed for a specific task. Antivirus / Anti-malware Scans files and programs for viruses, spyware, ransomware. Removes or quarantines threats. e.g. Windows Defender, Norton, Avast Disk Defragmenter Reorganises fragmented data on HDD so files are stored in contiguous blocks — loads faster. NOT used on SSDs. e.g. Windows Disk Defragmenter File Compression Reduces file size for storage or transfer by encoding data more efficiently. e.g. WinZip, 7-Zip — creates .zip files Backup Software Automatically copies data to a safe location so it can be restored if the original is lost or damaged. e.g. Time Machine (Mac), Windows Backup Disk Cleanup Removes temporary files, cached browser data, and junk files to free up storage space. e.g. Windows Disk Cleanup, CCleaner Firewall Monitors and filters incoming and outgoing network traffic; blocks unauthorised connections. e.g. Windows Firewall, ZoneAlarm Encryption Software Encodes data so it can only be read by authorised users who possess the decryption key. e.g. BitLocker, VeraCrypt System Monitor Displays real-time CPU, RAM, disk, and network usage; helps diagnose performance problems. e.g. Windows Task Manager, Activity Monitor (Mac) Device Driver Manager Installs, updates and manages driver software that lets the OS communicate with hardware devices. e.g. Windows D"
