    "heading": "8. Common Mistakes",
    "id": "h-8",
    "text": "✗ COMMON MISTAKES ✗ Classifying antivirus as application software — it is a UTILITY (system software). It maintains the computer, not the user's work. ✗ Only answering 'why' for spec 1.3.7 — the spec asks for WHY, HOW and RISKS. Missing HOW and RISKS costs marks every time. ✗ Omitting communication software (spec 1.3.6) from revision entirely — it is a standalone spec point worth 2–3 marks per question. ✗ Only giving one purpose of communication software — the spec lists TWO: (1) exchange messages/files, and (2) provide remote access. ✗ Confusing open source with 'always free' — open source means the SOURCE CODE is publicly available; some open source licences restrict commercial use. ✗ Saying 'it is better' or 'it is faster' without explaining HOW or WHY — the second mark always requires a developed explanation. ✗ Listing update risks without explaining consequences — 'data loss' scores 0; 'the update may fail mid-installation, corrupting files' scores 1. ✗ Missing control software and project management from spec 1.3.4 — both are listed in the spec and will be tested in scenario questions. ✗ Not linking scenario answers to the context — 'custom software is expensive' is generic; 'custom software would cost too much for a school with a tight budget' is specific. ✗ Describing convergence between system and application software — this is a Chapter 1 concept. In Chapter 2, the key distinction is OS vs utility vs application."
