    "heading": "8. Recordable (R) vs Rewritable (R/W) [Spec 1.5.5]",
    "id": "h-8",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 1.5.5 Know that storage media can be recordable/write once (R) and rewritable (R/W). Optical discs come in two types. This is a frequently tested distinction because it determines whether a disc can be reused. Recordable (R) — Write Once Rewritable (R/W) — Reusable Data can only be written ONCE — cannot be erased or overwritten Data can be written, erased and rewritten many times Once written, the disc is permanently 'read-only' Works like a reusable storage device — can be formatted and reused Cheaper to manufacture than R/W discs Slightly more expensive but far more flexible Good for permanent archiving or software distribution Good for backups, temporary storage, frequent updates Cannot be used if you make a mistake — the data is permanent Mistakes can be corrected by erasing and rewriting Examples: CD-R, DVD-R, BD-R Examples: CD-RW, DVD-RW, BD-RE Typical use: music CD you burn once; archive of photos Typical use: backup disc updated weekly; reusable data transfer 📌 R vs R/W — All Formats at a Glance CD-R (Compact Disc Recordable) — Write once; cannot erase or overwrite. Cheap. Archive/distribute. CD-RW (Compact Disc Rewritable) — Erase and rewrite up to ~1,000 times. Reusable backup medium. DVD-R / DVD+R Write once DVD; 4.7GB capacity. Film distribution; software installation; archival. DVD-RW / DVD+RW Rewritable DVD; same capacity. Reusable backup; video production. BD-R Blu-ray Recordable — Write once; 25–100GB. 4K film distribution; long-term data archival. BD-RE Blu-ray Rewritable — Reusable; same capacity. Professional video backup; master copies. Exam tip: If a question mentions 'cannot be altered after recording' or 'permanent archive' — think Recordable (R). If 'updated weekly' or 'reused' — think Rewritable (R/W)."
