    "heading": "9. Storage Capacity Units [Spec 1.5.6]",
    "id": "h-9",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 1.5.6 Understand the terms describing the capacity of storage: bit, byte, KiB, mebibytes, gibibytes, tebibytes — using 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes. The spec uses binary prefixes (KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB) rather than the more common decimal prefixes (KB, MB, GB, TB). The difference is the base: binary uses 1,024 while decimal uses 1,000. 9.1 The Capacity Scale — Smallest to Largest b Bit 1 binary digit (0 or 1) → Smallest unit of data B Byte 8 bits → One character of text (e.g. the letter 'A') KiB Kibibyte 1,024 bytes (2^10) → A very short text file or email MiB Mebibyte 1,024 KiB = 1,048,576 bytes → A high-quality photograph; 1 minute of audio GiB Gibibyte 1,024 MiB = 1,073,741,824 bytes → About 1 hour of HD video; 200 music tracks TiB Tebibyte 1,024 GiB = 1,099,511,627,776 bytes → About 500 hours of HD video; entire film libraries 9.2 Conversion Reference Table Unit Value How Many Bits? Real-World Size 1 bit 1 binary digit (0 or 1) 1 bit Single on/off state in memory 1 Byte (B) 8 bits 8 bits One character of text (e.g. letter 'E') 1 Kibibyte (KiB) 1,024 Bytes 8,192 bits Short email; 1,024 characters of text 1 Mebibyte (MiB) 1,024 KiB = 1,048,576 Bytes ~8.4 million bits 1 minute of MP3 audio; 1 high-quality photo 1 Gibibyte (GiB) 1,024 MiB = 1,073,741,824 Bytes ~8.6 billion bits ~700 photos; ~1 hour HD video; ~200 songs 1 Tebibyte (TiB) 1,024 GiB = 1,099,511,627,776 Bytes ~8.8 trillion bits ~700,000 photos; ~500 hours of HD video 9.3 Binary (KiB) vs Decimal (KB) — Why It Matters Binary Prefix (Spec uses these) Decimal Prefix (Common in marketing) KiB = Kibibyte = 1,024 bytes KB = Kilobyte = 1,000 bytes MiB = Mebibyte = 1,048,576 bytes MB = Megabyte = 1,000,000 bytes GiB = Gibibyte = 1,073,741,824 bytes GB = Gigabyte = 1,000,000,000 bytes TiB = Tebibyte = 1,099,511,627,776 bytes TB = Terabyte = 1,000,000,000,000 bytes Used in OS, RAM specifications, file systems Used in hard drive advertising, consumer products Why it matters: A '1 TB' hard drive (decimal) shows as ~931 GiB in Windows — this is why drives always seem smaller than advertised Manufacturers use decimal (larger numbers look bigger); OS uses binary (which is more accurate for actual data handling) 🧮 Capacity Calculation Examples — Exam Practice Q: A file is 4 MiB. How many bytes is this? 4 × 1,024 × 1,024 = 4,194,304 bytes Q: A USB drive holds 8 GiB. How many MiB is this? 8 × 1,024 = 8,192 MiB Q: A hard drive has 2 TiB capacity. How many GiB? 2 × 1,024 = 2,048 GiB Q: A school has 500 student files each 2 MiB. What total storage in GiB is needed? 500 × 2 MiB = 1,000 MiB ÷ 1,024 = 0.977 GiB (approximately 1 GiB) Exam tip: Always show your working. Use the formula: multiply UP (to larger units: x1,024). Divide DOWN (to smaller units: ÷1,024)."
