    "heading": "11. Model Answers",
    "id": "h-11",
    "text": "Study the structure of each answer. Notice how each point is Named → Explained → Linked to the scenario. ✎ MODEL ANSWER [3 marks] Q: A school uses optical mark recognition (OMR) to mark multiple-choice exam papers. Explain how OMR works and give ONE advantage of using it. [3 marks] OMR works by shining a beam of light onto a pre-printed answer form (1). Where the student has made a pencil mark, less light is reflected back (1). The reader detects this difference in reflectance and records which position on the form was marked, identifying the student's chosen answer. One advantage is that OMR can mark thousands of exam papers automatically without human input (1), which saves the school significant time and reduces the chance of marking errors compared to manual checking. Why this scores full marks: ✓ HOW OMR works explained: light, mark detection, reflectance — not just 'it scans the paper' ✓ Advantage given with a consequence: 'automatic' AND 'saves time' — develops the point for the second mark ✓ Answer demonstrates understanding of WHY OMR is used in this context (exams), not just what it does generally ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Q: Explain TWO advantages of using a solid state drive (SSD) instead of a hard disk drive (HDD) for a laptop computer. [4 marks] 1. An SSD has no moving parts (1), which makes it much more durable and resistant to physical damage from drops or bumps — particularly important for a laptop that is carried between locations (1). 2. An SSD has significantly faster read and write speeds than an HDD (1), which means the laptop's operating system boots more quickly and files open almost instantly, improving productivity (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Two distinct advantages — not variations of the same point ✓ Advantage 1: no moving parts → explains consequence (durability) → linked to laptop context (carried around) ✓ Advantage 2: faster speeds → explains consequence (quick boot, instant file opening) → linked to user benefit ✓ Each advantage earns 2 marks: the feature (1) + the explained consequence (1) ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Q: A photographer takes 200 high-resolution photos during an event. She needs to transfer them to her laptop. Identify a suitable storage medium and explain TWO reasons why it is suitable. [4 marks] An SD card (Secure Digital card) would be suitable. Firstly, SD cards are built into most digital cameras (1), which means the photographer does not need any additional equipment to store the photos — they are captured directly onto the card during the shoot (1). Secondly, SD cards are compact and portable (1), allowing the photographer to physically carry the card to her laptop and transfer the images quickly using a card reader, without needing cables or a network connection (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Device specifically named ('SD card' — not just 'memory card') ✓ Reason 1 linked to scenario: built into camera → no extra equipment needed at the event ✓ Reason 2 linked to scenario: portability → easy transfer to laptop without cables ✓ Both reasons are scenario-specific, not generic advantages of SD cards ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Q: Explain the difference between a storage device and storage media. Give one example of each. [4 marks] A storage DEVICE is the physical mechanism that reads and/or writes data — it contains the motors, arms, lasers or electronic circuits that perform the data transfer (1). Example: the hard disk drive (HDD) mechanism — the assembly of spinning platters, motor and read/write arm (1). Storage MEDIA is the physical material on which data is actually stored — it retains the data as magnetic, optical or electronic patterns even when power is removed (1). Example: the magnetic platter inside the HDD, or a CD/DVD disc — the surface that holds the data (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Device: mechanism that reads/writes — clear definition not just 'the device' ✓ Media: material that stores the data — clear definition emphasising the 'storage' function ✓ Both examples linked to the same system (HDD) to clearly show the distinction ✓ Each definition (1) and each example (1) earns one mark — four marks total"
