    "heading": "9. Model Answers",
    "id": "h-9",
    "text": "Each model answer shows the exact structure needed: Characteristic → Explanation → Consequence. Notice how every answer is developed beyond one sentence. ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Q: Describe TWO differences between RAM and ROM. [4 marks] 1. RAM is volatile (1) — all data stored in RAM is immediately lost when the computer is switched off, because it requires a constant power supply to maintain its contents (1). 2. RAM can be both read from and written to by the CPU (1) — programs and data are constantly being changed in RAM as the user works; whereas ROM is read-only and its contents are fixed at the point of manufacture and cannot be altered by the user (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Two distinct differences identified — NOT two variations of 'one is bigger' ✓ Difference 1: Volatile — explained with 'because it requires a constant power supply' — the mechanism earns mark 2 ✓ Difference 2: Read/write vs read-only — explained with 'constantly being changed' and 'fixed at manufacture' ✓ Each answer earns 2 marks: the characteristic (1) + the explanation (1) ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Q: Explain the function of the CPU. Your answer should refer to the Fetch-Decode-Execute cycle. [4 marks] The CPU (Central Processing Unit) is the main processor of a computer — it carries out all program instructions (1). It does this by continuously repeating the Fetch-Decode-Execute cycle: First, it FETCHES the next instruction from RAM (1). The Control Unit then DECODES the instruction to determine what operation needs to be performed (1). Finally, the ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit) EXECUTES the instruction — performing the required calculation or data manipulation — and the result is stored back in RAM or a register (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ Overall function of CPU stated clearly first ✓ All three stages of the cycle named and explained — not just listed ✓ ALU and Control Unit both mentioned and linked to their correct stages ✓ Four separate marking points = four marks ✎ MODEL ANSWER [4 marks] Q: A primary school is choosing ICT equipment for a visually impaired student who has partial sight. Identify ONE hardware device and ONE software tool that would help this student and justify each choice. [4 marks] Hardware: A large high-resolution monitor (1) — a larger screen with high resolution allows text and images to be displayed at a bigger size without pixelation, making it easier for the partially sighted student to read content without straining (1). Software: Screen magnification software, such as the built-in Windows Magnifier (1) — this enlarges a selected portion of the screen up to several hundred percent, allowing the student to zoom in on any content (such as reading tasks or websites) without needing to change the display settings permanently (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ One hardware AND one software — both asked for and both provided ✓ Hardware justified: links the large monitor to the student's specific visual impairment — not just 'better to see' ✓ Software justified: explains the mechanism (zooms in) AND the benefit in the student's school context ✓ Each choice earns 2 marks: the correct identification (1) + the explanation linked to the scenario (1) ✎ MODEL ANSWER [3 marks] Q: Explain why having more RAM generally improves the performance of a computer. [3 marks] More RAM means the computer can hold more programs and data in fast working memory simultaneously (1). This means the CPU can access the data it needs almost instantly from RAM, rather than having to retrieve it from the much slower storage drive (1). When RAM is full, the OS must use virtual memory — space on the HDD/SSD — as overflow, which is significantly slower and causes the computer to slow down or freeze; more RAM prevents this from happening (1). Why this scores full marks: ✓ First mark: more programs held simultaneously in fast memory ✓ Second mark: CPU access to RAM vs slow storage drive — explains the mechanism ✓ Third mark: virtual memory explained as the consequence of insufficient RAM — the most developed point ✓ Three separate marks earned by three distinct, developed explanations"
