   {
    "heading": "13. Quick Reference — Key Terms & Revision",
    "id": "h-13",
    "text": "★ KEY TERMS — QUICK REFERENCE RAM (Random Access Memory): Volatile working memory — stores currently running programs and open files; lost when power off ROM (Read Only Memory): Permanent, non-volatile memory containing fixed start-up instructions (BIOS/UEFI); cannot be changed by users Volatile Memory: Memory that loses all data immediately when power is removed (RAM is volatile) Non-volatile Memory: Memory that retains data without power (ROM, flash memory, HDD, SSD are all non-volatile) Flash Memory: Non-volatile electronic storage with no moving parts; used in SSDs, USB drives, SD cards, smartphones BIOS / UEFI: Basic Input/Output System / Unified Extensible Firmware Interface — the start-up instructions stored in ROM that run before the OS loads CPU (Central Processing Unit): The main processor of the computer; carries out all instructions via the Fetch-Decode-Execute cycle ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit): Component inside the CPU that performs all arithmetic calculations and logical comparisons Control Unit (CU): Component inside the CPU that fetches, decodes and coordinates execution of all instructions Register: Tiny, extremely fast storage location inside the CPU that holds data being actively processed Cache: Very fast memory inside or near the CPU (L1/L2/L3) that stores recently used data to reduce RAM access Clock Speed: The number of instruction cycles a CPU completes per second, measured in GHz (gigahertz) GHz (Gigahertz): 1 GHz = 1 billion clock cycles per second; higher GHz generally = faster processing Multi-core Processor: A CPU containing more than one independent processing core; allows true parallel processing of multiple tasks Fetch-Decode-Execute Cycle: The fundamental process by which the CPU processes instructions: fetch from RAM → decode → execute → store result Virtual Memory: Space on a storage drive used as temporary overflow when RAM is full; much slower than real RAM Accessibility: The design of ICT systems and settings to ensure they can be used by people with physical, sensory or cognitive needs Screen Reader: Software that converts text displayed on screen to synthesised speech; used by blind and visually impaired users RAID: Redundant Array of Independent Disks — uses multiple storage drives together for speed or to prevent data loss if one drive fails ⚡ Memory Types — Quick Comparison for Revision RAM: Volatile │ Read/Write │ Fast │ Temporary │ Working memory │ 4–128 GB │ Lost at shutdown ROM: Non-volatile │ Read-only │ Fast │ Permanent │ Start-up only │ Few MB │ Never changes Flash Memory: Non-volatile │ Read/Write │ Fast │ Permanent │ Storage │ Varies │ Retained without power Cache: Volatile │ Read/Write │ Extremely fast │ Tiny (MB) │ CPU-side │ Holds recent instructions Virtual Memory: Non-volatile (on drive) │ Slow │ Used when RAM full │ Not real memory │ Causes slowdown CPU Performance Factors: Clock speed (GHz) + Number of cores + Cache size + RAM amount + Bus speed + Storage type"
   }
