    "heading": "8. How Devices Are Identified on a Network [Spec 2.3.1]",
    "id": "h-8",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 2.3.1 Know about network operating systems and how devices are identified: device name, IP address, MAC address. Device Name ↔ IP Address ↔ MAC Address Human-readable label given to a device on a network e.g. CLASSROOM-PC-07 Logical address assigned by router; used for routing across networks e.g. 192.168.1.47 Hardware address permanently coded into network card at manufacture e.g. 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E Feature IP Address MAC Address What it is A logical address assigned to a device on a network — can change A hardware address permanently burned into a network card at manufacture Format IPv4: Four groups of numbers 0–255 (e.g. 192.168.1.1) / IPv6: Eight hex groups Six pairs of hexadecimal digits (e.g. 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E) Set by Assigned by router (DHCP) or manually configured by admin Set permanently by the device manufacturer — cannot be changed Unique? Locally unique on a network; globally unique for public IPs Globally unique — no two devices have the same MAC address Changes? YES — can change each time a device connects to a network NO — permanently fixed in hardware Used for Routing data packets across networks and the internet Identifying devices on the same local network segment Who uses it Routers use IP to direct traffic across the internet Switches use MAC to direct traffic within a LAN Example School website server: 203.0.113.45 (public) / 192.168.1.50 (internal) Laptop network card: 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E — never changes ⚡ EXAM TIP 'Describe TWO ways a device can be identified on a network.' Answer: (1) IP address — a numerical address (e.g. 192.168.1.47) assigned by the router that identifies the device on the network and allows routers to direct data packets to the correct destination (1). (2) MAC address — a unique hardware address permanently embedded in the device's network interface card at manufacture (e.g. 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E) that identifies the device on the local network segment (1)."
