    "heading": "6. Presentations [Spec 6.5]",
    "id": "h-7",
    "text": "📋 SPEC 6.5 Master slide; placeholders; footer; slide number; background; font enhancements; insert text/images/action buttons/hyperlinks; animation; transitions; print options. Skill Area What to Do Key Points Master Slide / Template Set consistent background, fonts, colours, logo across all slides; add placeholder positions Master slide changes apply to ALL slides automatically — change once, affects all Slide Placeholders Positions on the slide for title, content, footer, page number, date Placeholders ensure consistent layout; text goes inside placeholder boundaries Inserting Content Text, images, action buttons (link to slide/URL), hyperlinks to external content Action buttons: Previous, Next, Home, Custom — make presentation interactive Animation Set individual elements to appear, move or exit during presentation Entrance effects (Appear, Fly In); Emphasis; Exit effects; set trigger and timing Slide Transitions Visual effect when moving from one slide to the next Apply sparingly; Fade and Wipe are professional; avoid random/excessive transitions Printing Options Handouts (2 or 3 slides per page); Notes pages; Full page slides; Headers and footers on printouts 3-per-page handout has lines for audience notes; include slide number and date in footer Footer / Slide Number Add consistent information to every slide footer Slide number; date; presentation title — set in Insert > Header and Footer 📌 Action Buttons vs Hyperlinks vs Animations — Know the Difference Hyperlink: Links text or object to another slide, file or URL — navigates when clicked during slideshow. Action button: Pre-designed clickable button (Next, Previous, Home, Custom) — appears as a shape with built-in navigation action. Animation: Applies an effect to a single object ON THE SLIDE (it appears, moves, or disappears during the presentation). Transition: Applies an effect BETWEEN slides — the visual effect as one slide disappears and the next appears. Exam trap: 'Add an animation to make the title appear' = Animation (on the slide element). 'Add a transition between slide 1 and slide 2' = Transition (between slides). Never confuse these. Print Option Pages per Sheet Notes When to Use Full Page Slides 1 slide per page Full size; highest quality; maximum visual impact When quality of visuals must be assessed; single slide per page handouts Handout (2 per page) 2 slides per page Side by side; smaller; still readable When distributing slides efficiently; audience needs to see detail Handout (3 per page) 3 slides per page Left column slides; right column blank lines for notes Most common handout format — audience writes notes next to each slide Handout (6 per page) 6 slides per page Very small; overview only Quick reference overview; summary of a long presentation Notes Pages 1 slide per page + speaker notes below Full slide image + presenter notes visible Presenter reference; printing notes to read from while presenting"
